Now I see the issue! In `console::Term::read_line_initial_text` at line 351-356:

```rust
Key::Char(chr) => {
    chars.push(chr);
    let mut bytes_char = [0; 4];
    chr.encode_utf8(&mut bytes_char);
    slf.write_str(chr.encode_utf8(&mut bytes_char))?;
    slf.flush()?;
}
```

Note that `Key::Char(chr)` matches ALL `Key::Char` variants including control characters like `\x04` (Ctrl+D). 

This is different from dialoguer's `interact_text` which filters with `Key::Char(chr) if !chr.is_ascii_control()`. So in the console crate's `read_line_initial_text`, Ctrl+D would be treated as a regular character and added to the string. But the bivvy code uses `Input::interact_on`, not `Input::interact_text`, which calls `term.read_line()` and ultimately `read_line_initial_text("")` with `term.read_key()`.

So when Ctrl+D is pressed, `read_key()` returns the control character, it gets pushed into the input string, written to the terminal, and the loop continues until Enter is pressed—no panic there either. Let me check what happens in MultiSelect, and more broadly, maybe the panic is coming from somewhere else in the code rather than the prompt reading itself.

